==================================================================
DAY 19 - PART A: THEORY
Ingress and Ingress Controllers
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. THE PROBLEM
--------------
10 web apps with 10 LoadBalancer Services = 10 cloud load balancers =
10 bills and 10 IPs. You want ONE entry point that routes by:
  host:   shop.example.com  -> shop Service
          api.example.com   -> api Service
  path:   example.com/      -> frontend
          example.com/api   -> backend
plus TLS termination in one place.


2. INGRESS = RULES, CONTROLLER = THE ENGINE
-------------------------------------------
Ingress (object)       Only a set of HTTP routing rules. Does nothing
                       by itself.
Ingress controller     A reverse proxy running in the cluster (NGINX,
                       Traefik, HAProxy, cloud ALB) that watches
                       Ingress objects and configures itself.

  client -> LB/NodePort -> ingress controller pods -> Service -> pods

Without a controller installed, Ingress objects are ignored.


3. INGRESS YAML
---------------
  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
  kind: Ingress
  metadata:
    name: shop
  spec:
    ingressClassName: nginx
    rules:
    - host: shop.local
      http:
        paths:
        - path: /
          pathType: Prefix
          backend:
            service:
              name: frontend
              port:
                number: 80
        - path: /api
          pathType: Prefix
          backend:
            service:
              name: backend
              port:
                number: 80
    tls:
    - hosts: [shop.local]
      secretName: shop-tls     # Week 8 with cert-manager

pathType:
  Prefix   /api matches /api, /api/, /api/users
  Exact    only exactly /api

ingressClassName picks which controller handles this Ingress (you can
run more than one).


4. CONTROLLER-SPECIFIC FEATURES
-------------------------------
Rewrites, rate limits, auth, timeouts are set with ANNOTATIONS that
are specific to each controller, e.g.
  nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /


5. IMPORTANT NEWS: INGRESS-NGINX AND GATEWAY API
------------------------------------------------
The community "ingress-nginx" controller was retired in 2026 (no more
updates or security fixes). It still works fine for LEARNING, and
millions of clusters still run it, so this lab uses it. For new
production work, teams choose either:
  - another Ingress controller (Traefik, HAProxy, F5 NGINX, cloud
    controllers like AWS Load Balancer Controller), or
  - the GATEWAY API: the successor to Ingress. Objects: GatewayClass,
    Gateway (the entry point), HTTPRoute (the rules). More expressive
    and role-oriented.
The concepts you learn today (host/path routing, TLS termination,
controller watches objects) carry over directly.


6. IN THE CLOUD (WEEK 7)
------------------------
Either: one LoadBalancer Service in front of the ingress controller,
or a cloud controller that turns each Ingress into a cloud application
load balancer (AWS ALB, GCP HTTP(S) LB, Azure App Gateway).


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Ingress = rules; controller = proxy that applies them.
- Route by host and path; terminate TLS once.
- Gateway API is the modern successor.
